<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Yucatán</h1><p class="st-meta">1542 CE – 1821 CE · capital Mérida</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Spanish colonial province of the Yucatán peninsula, governed from Mérida, where Maya communities kept much of their language and identity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After two failed attempts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-younger">Francisco de Montejo the Younger</a> conquered western Yucatán and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merida-yucatan">Mérida</a> in 1542 on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tho">T'ho</a>. Campaigns of 1543-46 subdued the eastern provinces, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-maya-revolt">Great Maya Revolt</a> of 1546-47 was crushed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Yucatán was governed by a governor and captain-general with considerable autonomy within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a>. With no precious metals, its economy rested on Maya tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth, wax and honey, and later on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> estates and logwood. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> led evangelisation; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Bishop Diego de Landa</a> burnt Maya books in 1562. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco-de-campeche">Campeche</a> was its fortified port, often raided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, and Maya revolts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacinto-canek">Jacinto Canek's</a> in 1761 recurred.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Yucatán became an intendancy in 1786 and joined independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1821. Its strong regional identity later produced attempts at secession and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste-war-of-yucatan">Caste War of Yucatán</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-yucatan.jpg" alt="Province of Yucatán" width="237" height="375"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Intendencia_de_yucat%C3%A1n.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1542 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mérida</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco de Montejo the Younger</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish, Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Captaincy General of Yucatán, Yucatan (colonial)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=province-of-yucatan" aria-label="Province of Yucatán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2270 1402 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Province of Yucatán lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2590 1658L2592 1679M2590 1658L2603 1672M2590 1658L2574 1680M2590 1658L2600 1654M2590 1658L2599 1664M2590 1658L2592 1666M2590 1658L2618 1686M2590 1658L2563 1677M2590 1658L2608 1748M2590 1658L2548 1716M2590 1658L2658 1678M2590 1658L2537 1761M2590 1658L2321 1717M2590 1658L4910 989M2590 1658L2321 1717"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2592 1679h0M2603 1672h0M2574 1680h0M2600 1654h0M2599 1664h0M2592 1666h0M2618 1686h0M2563 1677h0M2608 1748h0M2548 1716h0M2658 1678h0M2537 1761h0M2321 1717h0M4910 989h0M2321 1717h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2590 1658h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2590 1658h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mérida. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=province-of-yucatan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
